WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.911
True position, part two. Datums, and what position

2
00:00:05.011 --> 00:00:08.120
controls with one, two or three of them.

3
00:00:10.833 --> 00:00:14.142
Every basic dimension starts from a datum. But

4
00:00:14.242 --> 00:00:17.033
a real surface is never perfectly flat.

5
00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:20.652
So the datum isn't the surface itself. It's the perfect

6
00:00:20.752 --> 00:00:24.421
plane of the inspection equipment the part rests on, like

7
00:00:24.521 --> 00:00:27.726
a surface plate. The part sits on its high points.

8
00:00:27.816 --> 00:00:31.274
On the print, a triangle and a lettered box mark

9
00:00:31.374 --> 00:00:35.646
the surface the datum comes from, called the datum feature.

10
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:41.503
Start with position to just one datum. This hole goes straight

11
00:00:41.603 --> 00:00:45.273
through face A, so datum A is square to the hole.

12
00:00:45.366 --> 00:00:48.624
With only A in the frame, nothing ties the zone to

13
00:00:48.724 --> 00:00:52.049
the edges of the part. The zone has to stand square

14
00:00:52.149 --> 00:00:55.406
to A, but it's free to slide wherever the hole is.

15
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.632
To calculate it, measure the hole's axis at the top and at

16
00:00:59.732 --> 00:01:04.522
the bottom. Here the top sits six thousandths over from the bottom.

17
00:01:04.622 --> 00:01:08.900
The smallest zone that stays square to A and still holds the

18
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.520
axis is six thousandths across. Position is diameter six thousandths, a pass.

19
00:01:14.616 --> 00:01:18.191
With a single hole, position to A alone only

20
00:01:18.291 --> 00:01:23.536
controls tilt. It works exactly like a perpendicularity callout.

21
00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:28.471
Position to A alone still matters for a pattern. Here

22
00:01:28.571 --> 00:01:32.815
are four holes, with basic dimensions between the holes, and

23
00:01:32.915 --> 00:01:36.653
the frame says four X, position ten thousandths to A.

24
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:41.765
The four zones are locked to each other at the basic spacing, like a rigid

25
00:01:41.865 --> 00:01:46.190
gauge. But as a group, they can slide and turn to fit the holes.

26
00:01:46.283 --> 00:01:49.610
So the callout controls the spacing between the holes and

27
00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.677
keeps each one square to A. Where the whole pattern

28
00:01:52.777 --> 00:01:55.203
sits on the plate isn't controlled at all.

29
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.662
Now a different part: a hole drilled sideways, parallel

30
00:02:00.762 --> 00:02:03.360
to the bottom face A, one inch up.

31
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:08.160
Position to A locks the zone at exactly one inch above

32
00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:12.970
A, and parallel to it. Side to side, nothing holds it.

33
00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:17.564
To calculate it, measure the axis height above A at both ends

34
00:02:17.664 --> 00:02:22.238
of the hole. Plus three thousandths at the front, minus two at

35
00:02:22.338 --> 00:02:27.590
the back. Position is twice the worst end: diameter six thousandths. It

36
00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.736
passes, and the hole is parallel to A within that zone.

37
00:02:31.833 --> 00:02:35.786
Even if the hole were a quarter inch off sideways, this callout

38
00:02:35.886 --> 00:02:40.353
would still pass it. To control that direction, you need another datum.

39
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:46.346
To fix a hole's location completely, the part has to be locked down

40
00:02:46.446 --> 00:02:50.950
in all six ways it can move: three slides, and three rotations.

41
00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:54.288
Datum A is primary. The part sits down on the

42
00:02:54.388 --> 00:02:58.613
surface plate and touches at least three high points. That

43
00:02:58.713 --> 00:03:02.043
stops it sliding down, and tilting either way.

44
00:03:02.133 --> 00:03:07.157
Datum B is secondary. The part slides against the B wall and

45
00:03:07.257 --> 00:03:12.623
touches at least two points. That stops one slide, and the spin.

46
00:03:12.716 --> 00:03:17.853
Datum C is tertiary. The part slides over until it touches the C

47
00:03:17.953 --> 00:03:23.336
wall at one point. That stops the last slide. Now nothing can move.

48
00:03:23.433 --> 00:03:27.576
Three points, then two, then one. That's why it's called

49
00:03:27.676 --> 00:03:31.820
three, two, one. The datum reference frame is now fixed,

50
00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.003
and true position has an exact home in it.

51
00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.591
Back to the top view, with the part locked against B and

52
00:03:39.691 --> 00:03:43.520
C. Each coordinate is measured straight out from its own datum.

53
00:03:43.616 --> 00:03:47.887
The error from datum B is plus two thousandths in X. The

54
00:03:47.987 --> 00:03:52.570
error from datum C is plus one thousandth in Y. Combine them

55
00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:57.956
with the formula, and position is forty-five ten-thousandths. A pass.

56
00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:01.776
Datum A keeps the zone square to the face. Datums

57
00:04:01.876 --> 00:04:05.290
B and C each give one direction of the error.

58
00:04:06.483 --> 00:04:10.377
Datum order matters whenever the datum features aren't perfectly

59
00:04:10.477 --> 00:04:12.999
square to each other. On this part, edge C

60
00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:15.683
is ten thousandths out of square to edge B.

61
00:04:15.766 --> 00:04:19.106
With B before C, the part is squared up to edge

62
00:04:19.206 --> 00:04:22.620
B with two points, and C only touches at its low

63
00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.256
corner. Measured that way, position is forty-five ten-thousandths. It passes.

64
00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:32.488
Now swap them: C before B. The part is squared up to

65
00:04:32.588 --> 00:04:38.031
the crooked edge C instead, and the whole measuring frame turns with

66
00:04:38.131 --> 00:04:43.736
it. Same hole, but now delta X is about fifty-one ten-thousandths, and

67
00:04:43.836 --> 00:04:48.220
position comes out at about 11.6 thousandths. It fails.

68
00:04:48.316 --> 00:04:52.736
Same part, same hole, two different answers. Always set the part

69
00:04:52.836 --> 00:04:56.196
up in the exact order the frame lists the datums.

70
00:04:57.383 --> 00:05:01.180
Both editions of A S M E Y fourteen point five use

71
00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.779
these same datum ideas. Most of our prints call out the two

72
00:05:05.879 --> 00:05:11.313
thousand nine edition. One difference shows up when a datum face rocks.

73
00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.686
Under two thousand nine, a rocking face can leave a choice of

74
00:05:15.786 --> 00:05:21.295
candidate datums. The two thousand eighteen edition settles it by default: the

75
00:05:21.395 --> 00:05:25.898
part is adjusted to a single solution that minimizes the gap. It

76
00:05:25.998 --> 00:05:31.220
also renames the perfect theoretical gauge the true geometric counterpart.

77
00:05:31.316 --> 00:05:33.662
Always check the title block or notes to

78
00:05:33.762 --> 00:05:35.986
see which edition the print calls out.

79
00:05:36.683 --> 00:05:40.273
Next, in part three, we go into three D. The tolerance

80
00:05:40.373 --> 00:05:43.963
zone is really a cylinder, and the hole's tilt counts.
